<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.9.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02486</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.9.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ff943303-0f72-4033-9932-710247c39ba6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><exercise id="_73e4ba8b-5ba4-41b1-8314-aae3db1bcec7" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00002">Which of the following points is the vertex of the quadratic function \(f(x)=x^2+8x+22\)?</para>
      <para id="para-00003">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00004">\((8, 22)\)</para><para id="para-00005">\((0, 22)\)</para><para id="para-00006">\((-4, 6)\)</para><para id="para-00007">\((4, 6)\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00008">Correct! \((-4, 6)\)</para><para id="para-00009">\((\frac{b}{2})^2=(\frac{8}{2})^2=16\)
        \(f(x)=x^2+8x+16+22-16\)
        \(f(x)=x^2+8x+16+22-16\)
        \(f(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
        \(f(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
        Vertex: \((-4, 6)\)
        Vertex: \((-4, 6)\)</para><para id="para-00010">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.1.</para><para id="para-00011">The correct answer is \((-4, 6)\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_8585ee96-150f-4228-848f-ad2e0dccfa9c" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00012">For questions 2 – 4, use the function \(f(x)=(x-4)(x+2)\).</para>

      <para id="para-00013">Write the function in standard form.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00014">Correct! \(f(x)=x^2-2x-8\)</para><para id="para-00015">Expand:
      \(f(x)=(x-4)(x+2)\)
      \(f(x)=(x-4)(x+2)\)
      \(f(x)=x^2-4x+2x-8\)
      \(f(x)=x^2-4x+2x-8\)
      \(f(x)=x^2-2x-8\)
      \(f(x)=x^2-2x-8\)</para><para id="para-00016">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.2.</para><para id="para-00017">The correct answer is \(f(x)=x^2-2x-8\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_55fe7e4c-ebc6-43b7-96e9-5c03353479ae" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00018">Write the function from question 2 in vertex form.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00019">Correct! \(f(x)=(x-1)^2-9\)</para><para id="para-00020">Complete the square:
      \(f(x)=x^2 - 2x + 1 - 8 - 1\)
      \(f(x)=x^2 - 2x + 1 - 8 - 1\)
      \(f(x)=(x^2 - 2x + 1) - 9\)
      \(f(x)=(x^2 - 2x + 1) - 9\)
      \(f(x)=(x-1)^2 - 9\)
      \(f(x)=(x-1)^2 - 9\)</para><para id="para-00021">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.2.</para><para id="para-00022">The correct answer is \(f(x)=(x-1)^2-9\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_95479bf9-631e-4a04-ac3e-9a400994015d" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00023">Identify the vertex of the quadratic function \(f(x) = (x-1)^2 - 9\).</para>
    <para id="para-00024">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00025">\((1, -9)\)</para><para id="para-00026">\((1, 9)\)</para><para id="para-00027">\((-1, 9)\)</para><para id="para-00028">\((-9, -1)\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00029">Correct! The vertex is \((1, -9)\).</para><para id="para-00030">The function is written in vertex form:
      \(f(x) = (x - h)^2 + k\)
      \(f(x) = (x - h)^2 + k\)
      \(f(x) = (x - 1)^2 - 9\)
      \(f(x) = (x - 1)^2 - 9\)
      \(h = 1\) and \(k = -9\)
      \(h = 1\) and \(k = -9\)
      So, the vertex is \((1, -9)\).
    
      So, the vertex is \((1, -9)\).
   </para><para id="para-00031">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.1.</para><para id="para-00032">The correct answer is \((1, -9)\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_1ed28060-bc51-4003-91db-cfee70fddd12" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00005">
    <para id="para-00034">For questions 5 - 6, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-4, 1)\) and also goes through the point \((6, -1)\).</para>
      
      <para id="para-00035">Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form in simplest form.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00036">Correct! \( \frac{-1}{50} \)</para><para id="para-00037">\(-1=a(6-(-4))^2+1\)
        \(-1=a(10)^2+1\)
        \(-1=a(10)^2+1\)
        \(-2=100a\)
        \(-2=100a\)
        \(a=\frac{-2}{100}=\frac{-1}{50}\)
        \(a=\frac{-2}{100}=\frac{-1}{50}\)</para><para id="para-00038">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.3.</para><para id="para-00039">The correct answer is \( \frac{-1}{50} \).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5cb3fdfc-ced5-4ef0-ae95-b0c6c6541248" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00041">Write the equation in vertex form. Hint: Be sure to use y = to denote your equation.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00006"><para id="para-00042">Correct! \(y=\frac{-1}{50}(x+4)^2+1\)</para><para id="para-00043">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.2.</para><para id="para-00044">The correct answer is \(y=\frac{-1}{50}(x+4)^2+1\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="a801a69a-ce71-4fc4-8cf5-402892a8e844" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00007"><para id="para-00045">For questions 7 and 8, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-2, -3)\) and also goes through the point \((4, 2)\).</para><para id="para-00046">Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form in simplest form.</para>
    
      
  </problem><solution id="sol-00007"><para id="para-00048">Correct! \( \frac{5}{36} \)</para><para id="para-00049">\(2=a(4-(-2))^2+(-3)\)
        \(2=a(6)^2+(-3)\)
        \(2=a(6)^2+(-3)\)
        \(2+3=36a+(-3)+3\)
        \(2+3=36a+(-3)+3\)
        \(5=36a\)
        \(5=36a\)
        \(a=\frac{5}{36}\)
        \(a=\frac{5}{36}\)</para><para id="para-00050">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.3.</para><para id="para-00051">The correct answer is \(a=\frac{5}{36}\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_25dd1a17-d959-45a0-9466-755d9d347cff" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00008"><para id="para-00052">Write the equation in vertex form. Hint: Be sure to use y = to denote your equation.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00008"><para id="para-00053">Correct! \(y=\frac{5}{36}(x+2)^2-3\)</para><para id="para-00054">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.3.</para><para id="para-00055">The correct answer is \(y=\frac{5}{36}(x+2)^2-3\).</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_0ea78142-8d2b-4f7c-ad07-60bf616e0e8c" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00009"><para id="para-00056">For questions 9 - 10, use the function \(f(x)=x^2\) that is shifted to create the function \(g(x)\) that has a vertex at \((-4, 6)\).</para>
    <para id="para-00057">Write \(g(x)\) in vertex form.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00009"><para id="para-00058">Correct! \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)</para><para id="para-00059">Substitute the vertex in for \((h, k)\):
      \(g(x)=(x-(-4))^2+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x-(-4))^2+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)</para><para id="para-00060">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.3.</para><para id="para-00061">The correct answer is \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\).</para><para id="para-00062">Substitute the vertex: \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_60bd9f86-e40b-4662-9244-1f9ad22d5935" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00010"><para id="para-00063">Write \(g(x)\) in standard form.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00010"><para id="para-00064">Correct! \(g(x)=x^2+8x+22\)</para><para id="para-00065">Expand and simplify:
      \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)(x+4)+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)(x+4)+6\)
      \(g(x)=x^2+4x+4x+16+6\)
      \(g(x)=x^2+4x+4x+16+6\)
      \(g(x)=x^2+8x+22\)
      \(g(x)=x^2+8x+22\)</para><para id="para-00066">Try again. Revisit activity 9.9.3.</para><para id="para-00067">The correct answer is \(g(x)=x^2+8x+22\).</para><para id="para-00068">Steps:
      \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)^2+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)(x+4)+6\)
      \(g(x)=(x+4)(x+4)+6\)
      \(g(x)=x^2+8x+22\)
      \(g(x)=x^2+8x+22\)</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Interaction End --></section>
